    "problem_statement": "LM.__call__ method is broken and causes NameError\n\nDescription\n\nWhen using the LM class's __call__ method, it causes a NameError because it tries to use variables that haven't been defined yet. This happens when making direct calls to language models.\n\nTo reproduce the issue:\n\n```python\nimport dspy\nfrom dspy.clients.lm import LM\n\n# Create an LM instance\nlm = LM(model=\"openai/gpt-3.5-turbo\")\n\n# This will fail with a NameError\nresult = lm(\"What is the capital of France?\")\n```\n\nThe error occurs because the `__call__` method in `dspy/clients/lm.py` references variables like `response` and `outputs` before they are defined. This breaks all direct calls to language models.\n\nExpected behavior:\nThe LM.__call__ method should properly execute without any NameError and return the expected response from the language model."
